   "source": [
    "# Google Colab Connection for Seeruta App\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook provides instructions and code to connect your local Seeruta development environment with Google Colab for cloud-based development and execution.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Benefits of Using Google Colab\n",
    "- **Cloud computing**: Leverage free GPU/TPU resources\n",
    "- **No local setup**: Run code without installing dependencies locally\n",
    "- **Collaboration**: Share notebooks with teammates\n",
    "- **Backup**: Save your work to Google's cloud storage\n",
    "- **Access**: Work from any device with internet access\n",
    "\n",
    "## Prerequisites\n",
    "1. You have a Google account\n",
    "2. GitHub repository with Seeruta app code\n",
    "3. Basic familiarity with Python and Jupyter notebooks\n",
    "\n",
    "## Step 1: Fork the Repository\n",
    "1. Go to the Seeruta app GitHub repository\n",
    "2. Click the \"Fork\" button in the top-right corner\n",
    "3. This creates your own copy of the repository on GitHub\n",
    "\n",
    "## Step 2: Access in Google Colab\n",
    "Open [Google Colab](https://colab.research.google.com) in your web browser.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Step 3: Import Your Repository\n",
    "In Google Colab, go to File → New → Folder and create a folder for your project.\n",
    "\n",
    "To import your forked repository, use one of these methods:\n",
    "\n",
    "### Option A: Mount Google Drive\n",
    "```python\n",
    "from google.colab import drive\n",
    "\n",
    "# Mount your Google Drive (you\'ll be prompted to authorize)\n",
    "drive.mount('/content/drive')\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "Then navigate to your forked repository in your mounted drive.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Option B: Git Clone Directly\n",
    "```python\n",
    "!rm -rf /content/seeruta  # Remove if exists\n",
    "!git clone https://github.com/YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME/seeruta-app /content/seeruta\n",
    "!cd /content/seeruta\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "Replace `YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME` with your GitHub username.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Option C: Use Colab Pro with GitHub Access\n",
    "If you have Colab Pro, you can directly connect to your GitHub repository.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Step 4: Install Dependencies\n",
    "```python\n",
    "!pip install -r requirements.txt\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "If you don't have a `requirements.txt`, create one:\n",
    "```python\n",
    "# Create requirements.txt\n",
    "import os\n",
    "with open('requirements.txt', 'w') as f:\n",
    "    f.write('flutter==0.0.0\n')
    f.write('requests==2.31.0\n')
    f.write('pandas==2.0.3\n')
    "# Add other dependencies as needed\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "## Step 5: Set Up the Environment\n",
    "```python\n",
    "# Set up your working directory\n",
    "import os\n",
    "import sys\n",
    "\n",
    "os.chdir('/content/seeruta')\n",
    "sys.path.insert(0, '/content/seeruta')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Check if Flutter/Dart is available\n",
    "import subprocess\n",
    "result = subprocess.run(['flutter', '--version'], capture_output=True, text=True)\n",
    "if result.returncode == 0:\n",
    "    print('Flutter is available')\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    print('Flutter is not available. Install it manually.')\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "## Step 6: Basic Workflow\n",
    "Once connected, you can:\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1. Explore the Repository Structure\n",
    "```python\n",
    "import os\n",
    "\n",
    "# List all files and folders\n",
    "for item in os.listdir('.'):\n",
    "    print(item)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2. Run Development Commands\n",
    "```python\n",
    "# Run Flutter analyze\n",
    "!flutter analyze\n",
    "\n",
    "# Build the app\n",
    "!flutter build apk --debug\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3. Edit Files\n",
    "You can edit Python and other files directly in Colab using the notebook interface.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Step 7: Save Your Work\n",
    "1. **Save notebooks**: Go to File → Save notebook to drive\n",
    "2. **Commit changes**: Use Git commands to commit your work\n",
    "3. **Push changes**: Push changes back to your GitHub fork\n",
    "\n",
    "## Step 8: Troubleshooting\n",
    "\n",
    "**Common Issues:**\n",
    "1. **Permission denied when mounting Drive**: Make sure you're signed into the correct Google account\n",
    "2. **Git clone fails**: Check your internet connection and GitHub credentials\n",
    "3. **Missing dependencies**: Install them manually using pip\n",
    "\n",
    "## Step 9: Advanced Features\n",
    "\n",
    "### Version Control in Colab\n",
    "```python\n",
    "# Configure Git in Colab\n",
    "!git config --global user.email \"you@example.com\"\n",
    "!git config --global user.name \"Your Name\"\n",
    "\n",
    "# Add and commit changes\n",
    "!git add .\n",
    "!git commit -m \"Update from Colab\"\n",
    "\n",
    "# Push to your fork\n",
    "!git push origin main\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### Running Scripts\n",
    "```python\n",
    "# Run any Python script in the repository\n",
    "import subprocess\n",
    "subprocess.run(['python', 'your_script.py'], check=True)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "## Tips and Best Practices\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Version your notebooks**: Export important notebooks to .ipynb files for version control\n",
    "2. **Use checkpoints**: Periodically save your work\n",
    "3. **Monitor runtime**: Keep an eye on your Colab session time\n",
    "4. **Backup locally**: Don't rely solely on Colab for your code\n",
    "\n",
    "## Conclusion\n",
    "Using Google Colab with the Seeruta app allows you to leverage cloud computing resources while maintaining access to your local development environment. This setup is particularly useful for data processing, testing, and collaborative development.\n",
    "\n",
    "Happy coding! 🚀\n"
   ]
